    "statement0": "<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"sodium\" </i></span> is mentioned in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.0%</span> of papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. This puts in the bottom <span style=\"color: rgba(237, 12, 24, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">18<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of words.</p>Among papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"sodium\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">39.5%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are 'fragile' (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., just barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a much higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown in black); relative to other words, papers with <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"sodium\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">86<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages. Note, the strength of a paper's p-values strongly predict its replicability.",
